{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I’m Dr. Lee. I understand you came in with some epigastric pain. Can you tell me more about it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it started about a week ago. I had a cold—just congestion and a dry cough—and then this pressure in my upper belly began. It doesn’t move anywhere, and it’s not sharp, just a constant discomfort."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Does it get worse after eating? Or when you lie down?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little worse after meals, and I’ve been burping a lot. It feels gassy. But it goes away when I sleep. I took aspirin before coming because this pain… it’s the same one I had before my heart surgery last year."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:37:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important. Because of your history—CABG and prior heart issues—we take any recurring pain like this seriously. We’ve checked your cardiac enzymes three times, and all were negative. No sign of a heart attack."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, thank goodness. But why would it come back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your EKG showed minor changes—some T-wave inversion and ST depression—but no active ischemia. Given that you’re not having chest pain, shortness of breath, or sweating, and the labs are clear, we think this is likely related to acid reflux."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Heartburn? But it feels just like the pain before my heart attack."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And that’s why we admitted you. For patients with heart disease, stomach pain can be an ‘anginal equivalent’—meaning your heart issues can present as belly discomfort. But everything we’ve seen so far points away from your heart being the cause this time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay… so what’s next?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re on a heart monitor, and we’ve done a chest X-ray—no fluid in the lungs, heart size is normal. We’ll keep you overnight for observation. We’re continuing your usual meds: aspirin, metoprolol, atorvastatin, and lisinopril."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been really anxious lately—worried about money. Could that make the pain worse?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2165-09-15 02:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. Anxiety can amplify physical symptoms, especially stomach discomfort. We’ve noted that, and a chaplain visited you earlier. We’ll make sure you have support."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2165-09-15 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’ve been asymptomatic since admission, and your repeat labs are stable. Troponins stayed negative, kidney function is good. We’re confident this wasn’t a cardiac event."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2165-09-15 10:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s really just indigestion?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2165-09-15 10:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most likely, yes. Given your frequent burping and the timing with meals, we believe it’s gastroesophageal reflux. We gave you ranitidine in the hospital and recommend you start over-the-counter Prilosec when you go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2165-09-15 10:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can do that. Are any of my other medicines changing?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2165-09-15 10:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re switching your metoprolol to the extended-release version for smoother coverage, and we’re cutting your atorvastatin dose to 40 mg daily since your LDL came back at 41—excellent control. Everything else stays the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2165-09-15 10:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds manageable. And if the pain comes back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2165-09-15 10:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "If you have chest pain, shortness of breath, nausea, or the pain radiates, go to the ER immediately. But if it’s just belly discomfort with burping, try the Prilosec and see if it settles. Follow up with your primary doctor in a few days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2165-09-15 17:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. We’re clearing you to go home. Your condition is stable, and all signs point to resolved, non-cardiac epigastric pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2165-09-15 17:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much better knowing my heart’s okay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2165-09-15 17:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Keep taking your medications as prescribed, use the Prilosec for heartburn, and don’t hesitate to reach out if anything changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2165-09-15 17:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And thank you for explaining everything so clearly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2165-09-15 17:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up if needed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2165-09-15 02:16:00",
    "admission_end": "2165-09-15 18:06:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 70-year-old woman with a history of coronary artery disease, prior CABG, and hypothyroidism was admitted for epigastric pain that she recognized as a prior anginal equivalent. The pain began after a mild URI and was associated with bloating and burping, worsening slightly after meals but resolving with sleep. She denied chest pain, shortness of breath, nausea, or vomiting. Initial EKG showed minor changes including T-wave inversion in V1 and ST depression in V2, but cardiac enzymes were negative across three tests. She remained asymptomatic during telemetry monitoring. A chest X-ray showed no acute cardiopulmonary findings. The pain was attributed to gastroesophageal reflux, and she was managed with continued cardiac medications and addition of ranitidine during admission. At discharge, she was advised to start OTC Prilosec, continue her home regimen with adjustments including a switch to metoprolol succinate and reduced atorvastatin dose, and follow up with her primary care provider. Anxiety was noted during admission, and she received supportive care.",
    "problems": [
      "Abdominal pain, epigastric",
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of unspecified type of vessel, native or graft",
      "Aortocoronary bypass status",
      "Old myocardial infarction",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Osteoporosis, unspecified",
      "Anxiety state, unspecified",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Restless legs syndrome (RLS)"
    ]
  }
}
